rom ep-exdos13.rom page 0 cpu z80
default observed

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The extension ROM header's jumps and the action entry

@000A EXT_ENTRY
  The extension ROM's entry points after the "EXOS_ROM" header: $C00A is the action entry
  EXOS calls with C = the action code, $C00D and $C010 are entries for other code (the
  function dispatcher is $C010).
.000A Action entry: C = the action code
.000D Second entry: goes to $1C9F
.0010 EXDOS function call: A = the function number

@0013 EXT_ACTION
  Action entry (EXOS calls it with C = the action code, B, DE its parameters, IY = the RAM
  EXOS gave this ROM, page 1 = that RAM's segment).
  1. Adds $80 to IY, so IY-$80..IY+$7F reach the first 256 bytes of the workspace.
  2. Action 7 (RAM allocation) asks for the workspace (ACT7_RAM). Nothing else is done while
     page 1 holds segment 0 (no RAM yet), and only action 8 (initialise, INIT_ACTION) runs
     before bit 7 of IY-$37 marks the initialisation done.
  3. Action 1 (cold start) goes to ACT1_COLD, 2 (command string) to $2BF1, 3 (help string)
     to $2B5E, 4 (EXOS variable) to ACT4_VAR.
  4. Action 5 (explain error code, B = the code) takes codes $95-$BF (messages 0-$2A) and
     $CF (message $2B), unless EXDOS variable $50 (IY-$4E) is not 0; the message is built
     by MSG_TO_BUFFER and handed back with C = 0, B = $FF, DE = its address.
  Any other action returns with C unchanged, so EXOS offers it to the next extension.
.0014 IY + $80: the middle of the workspace's first 256 bytes
.0017 IY = workspace + $80
.001A A = the action code
.001B Action 7 (RAM allocation)?
.001F A = page 1's segment, the RAM EXOS gave this ROM
.0022 No RAM yet? Leave the action
.0024 Action 8 (initialise)?
.0029 Initialised (bit 7 of IY-$37)? Else leave the action
.002E Action 1 (cold start)?
.0032 Action 2 (command string)?
.0036 Action 3 (help string)?
.003A Action 4 (EXOS variable)?
.003D Action 5 (explain error code)? Others: leave
.003F EXDOS variable $50 not 0? Leave the explaining to others
.0043 B = the error code: $CF?
.0046 Error $CF has message $2B
.004A Codes $95-$BF have messages 0-$2A
.004D Below $95? Not ours
.004E Above $BF? Not ours

@0051 ACT5_EXPLAIN
  Action 5: builds message A in the workspace and hands it back to EXOS.
.0051 DE = the copy, A = its length
.0054 Empty message? Leave the action (C = 5)
.0056 C = 0: action taken; B = $FF, DE = the text

@005A ACT7_RAM
  Action 7 (RAM allocation): asks for $07D4 bytes of RAM with B = 1, and takes the action
  (C = 0).
.005A C = 0: taken; B = 1
.005D DE = $07D4: the bytes of RAM wanted

@0061 ACT4_VAR
  Action 4 (an EXOS variable from 40 up, E = its number; B = 0 read, 1 write, 2 toggle,
  D = the value to write).
  1. Variable $90: kept by EXDOS only when EXOS has none (IY+$6A = $FF, set at INIT_ACTION);
     its value is IY+$69.
  2. Variables $40-$63 are EXDOS's own: the bytes IY-$5E to IY-$3B ($40 is EXDOS's
     segment, $47 the current drive).
  Others are left for the next extension (C unchanged). VAR_ACCESS does the access and
  takes the action.
.0061 E = the variable's number
.0062 Variable $90?
.0066 Does EXDOS keep variable $90 (IY+$6A = $FF)?
.006B No: EXOS has it, leave the action
.006C A = the access: 0 read, 1 write, 2 toggle
.006D HL = IY+$69, variable $90's byte

@0077 ACT4_VAR_RANGE
  Variables $40-$63: HL = IY-$5E + (number - $40); then VAR_ACCESS.
.0077 Index from variable $40
.007A Below $40? Not ours
.007B Above $63? Not ours
.007E C = the index
.007F A = the access
.0082 HL = IY + index
.0086 - $5E: variable $40 is at IY-$5E

@008A VAR_ACCESS
  Reads, writes or toggles the variable byte at HL as the access code in A asks (0 read,
  1 write, 2 toggle), then returns its value in D with A = 0 and C = 0 (action taken).
.008A Access 0 (read)?
.008E Access 1 (write)?
.0090 Toggle: D = the byte inverted

@0093 VAR_WRITE
  Writes D to the variable byte, then reads it back.
.0093 Store the new value

@0094 VAR_READ
  Returns the variable's value: D = the byte at HL, A = 0, C = 0 (action taken).
.0094 D = the variable's value
.0095 A = 0, no error
.0096 C = 0: action taken

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Messages

@0098 MSG_TO_BUFFER
  Copies message A into the workspace as a counted text. Pages segment + 1 (EXDOS's second
  page) into page 1 and takes the message table whose address is at $4002 when EXOS
  variable $90 is 0, else the one at $4000. Each entry is a length byte and the text; every
  "\" in the text becomes CR LF. The copy at IY+$5DB is the length, the text and a 0.
  Entry: A = the message number; IY = the workspace.
  Exit: DE = the copy (IY+$5DB); A = its length; page 1 restored.
  Changes: A, DE.
  Keeps: F, BC, HL, IX, IY.
.009A E = the message number
.009B Keep page 1's segment
.009E A = this ROM's segment (page 3)
.00A0 The next segment: EXDOS's second page
.00A1 In page 1
.00A4 HL = the table at ($4002)
.00A7 Read EXOS variable $90
.00AD D = its value
.00AE Variable $90 = 0? Keep the table at ($4002)
.00B2 Else the table at ($4000)

@00B5 MSG_FIND
  Walks the table at HL to entry E (each entry a length byte and its text).
.00B5 E = the message number
.00B6 B = entry counter, D = 0 for the length

@00BA MSG_FIND_LP
  Skips one entry per pass until the counter in B reaches the message number in A.
.00BA This entry?
.00BD Next entry number
.00BE E = this entry's length
.00BF Skip the text
.00C0 And the length byte

@00C3 MSG_COPY_TEXT
  Copies the entry at HL to the buffer at IY+$5DB, after its length byte.
.00C3 BC = the entry
.00C5 A = its length
.00C9 HL = the buffer, IY+$5DB
.00CD Keep the buffer's address for the length
.00CE E = characters to copy + 1, D = the copy's length
.00D0 + 1 for the first DEC E

@00D1 MSG_COPY_LP
  Copies one character, ends when the count in E runs out.
.00D1 Next buffer byte
.00D2 Next text character
.00D3 All copied?
.00D7 "\"? It stands for a new line
.00DB Copy the character

@00DE MSG_BACKSLASH
  Writes CR LF for a "\": the copy grows by one byte.
.00DE CR
.00E1 LF
.00E3 One more byte in the copy

@00E6 MSG_COPY_END
  Ends the copy with a 0, writes its length in front, restores page 1 and returns
  A = the length, DE = the copy.
.00E6 A 0 after the text (E = 0)
.00E7 HL = the copy's start
.00E8 Its length byte
.00E9 Page 1's segment as it was
.00EC A = the copy's length
.00ED DE = the copy

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Small helpers

@00F2 IY_WORD_INLINE
  Reads a workspace word: DE = the word at IY + the signed byte that follows the CALL, and
  returns past that byte.
  Entry: the byte after the CALL = an offset from IY (-128 to 127); IY = the workspace.
  Exit: DE = the word at IY + the offset.
  Changes: DE.
  Keeps: AF, BC, HL, IX, IY.
.00F2 HL = the return address
.00F3 E = the inline byte
.00F4 Return past it
.00F7 HL = IY, the caller's HL kept on the stack
.00FA D = the byte's sign
.00FE HL = IY + the offset
.00FF DE = the word there

@0105 PATH_SEP_CHAR
  Returns the character that separates the names in a path (the callers look for it in a
  name): "\" ($5C) when EXOS variable $90 is not 0, "'" ($27) when it is 0.
  Entry: none.
  Exit: A = the separator.
  Changes: A.
  Keeps: F, BC, DE, HL, IX, IY.
.0108 Read EXOS variable $90
.010E D = its value
.010F Variable $90 = 0?
.0113 Not 0: "\"

@0117 SEP_QUOTE
  Variable $90 is 0: the separator is "'".
.0117 "'"

@0119 SEP_RETURN
  Returns the separator in A with the caller's flags, BC and DE.
.0119 The caller's flags
.011A A = the separator

@011E ACT1_COLD
  Action 1 (cold start), once (bit 6 of IY-$37): sets bits 6 and 3 of IY-$37, then has
  ANNOUNCE_DRIVES offer "EXDOS E:", "EXDOS F:", "EXDOS B:" and "EXDOS A:" to the
  extensions. C stays 1, so the other extensions get the action too.
.011E A = EXDOS's flags
.0121 Cold start done before?
.0124 Mark it done; bit 3 also set
.012A Offer the drive commands
.012D Room for a longer call

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Initialisation (action 8)

@013A INIT_ACTION
  Action 8 (initialise).
  1. Makes DISK the default device (EXOS 19, type 1); clears the error hook's segment
     (IY-$3A), IY-$80 and IY-$20; sets EXDOS variables $4B and $4C and IY+$2E to $FF.
  2. Already initialised (bit 7 of IY-$37)? The rest is REINIT's.
  3. First time: variable $47 (the current drive) becomes 1, variables $46 and $53 $FF,
     IY+$2E $FE; keeps page 0's segment (IY+$2C) and EXDOS's segment (IY+$2F and
     variable $40).
  4. Copies the 72 bytes at $04BD (RAM_FARCALL, RAM_SEGCALL and two device descriptors) to
     IY+$01ED and patches them: both CALL $0000s call the copy's JP (HL), and EXDOS's
     segment goes into the page-3 restore and both descriptors' table segments.
  5. Asks for 3 sector buffers and links the DISK device and the buffers (LINK_DISK_DEV);
     maps every drive to itself (FN26_MAP_DRIVE with B = 0).
  6. Adds the built-in units: 4 driven by $1FD3 (records from IY+$2BB) and, when that worked,
     makes drive 2 a copy of drive 1 (FN25_COPY_DRIVE); then 1 driven by $1005 (record at
     IY+$441). Both are in this segment, IX = IY for them.
  Goes on at INIT_LAST_UNIT.
.013A DE = "DISK", the default device's name
.013D C = the device type
.0141 No error hook (its segment = 0)
.014B A = $FF
.014C EXDOS variable $4B = $FF
.014F EXDOS variable $4C = $FF
.0155 Initialised before?
.015A Yes: only relink and restart
.015D EXDOS variable $47 (current drive) = 1
.0160 EXDOS variable $46 = $FF
.0163 EXDOS variable $53 = $FF
.0166 IY+$2E = $FE
.0169 Page 0's segment
.016E EXDOS's segment (page 3)
.0173 Is EXDOS variable $40
.0179 Workspace kept twice for later
.017B DE = IY+$01ED, where the RAM code goes
.0180 HL = the code and descriptors to copy
.0184 72 bytes
.0189 IX = the copy
.018B Page 3's segment the copy puts back
.018E HL = the copy's JP (HL) ($04D4's copy)
.0192 RAM_FARCALL's CALL goes to it
.0198 RAM_SEGCALL's CALL goes to it
.019E The second descriptor's table segment
.01A1 The DISK descriptor's table segment
.01A4 3 sector buffers wanted
.01A8 Link DISK and the buffers
.01AB B = 0
.01AD Every drive mapped to itself
.01B0 IX = IY, the workspace for the units' driver
.01B2 DE = the driver routine ($1FD3)
.01B5 Its segment: this ROM
.01B7 4 units, records from HL (IY+$2BB)
.01BC Drive 2 = a copy of drive 1, if the units were added
.01C2 HL = IX = IY
.01C6 HL = IY+$43E: one more unit's block
.01CA DE = the driver routine ($1005)
.01CF 1 unit

@01D1 INIT_LAST_UNIT
  Adds the last built-in unit (ADD_UNITS), then checks that EXOS has variable $90: if it
  has not, EXDOS keeps it itself (IY+$6A = $FF), with the value $FF (IY+$69).
.01D1 Add the unit
.01D7 Assume EXOS has variable $90
.01DB Read EXOS variable $90
.01E1 Exists? EXOS keeps it
.01E3 No: EXDOS answers for it (ACT4_VAR)
.01E7 Its value: $FF

@01EB INIT_MARK_DONE
  Marks the initialisation done (bit 7 of IY-$37).
.01EE Initialised

@01F2 INIT_SCAN_DRVS
  Offers the command "EXDOS" + $FF to the extensions (EXOS 26) until none takes it; each
  one that does hands back B = a segment and DE = a block of units, added by
  FN24_ADD_DRIVER.
.01F2 DE = the command "EXDOS" + $FF
.01F7 Nobody took it? Done
.01F9 Add the units it gave

@01FE INIT_ANNOUNCE
  First initialisation done: announces "EXDOS" + $FC with A = 0.
.01FE DE = the command "EXDOS" + $FC
.0201 A = 0 for EXDOS_STATE_RST

@0202 ANNOUNCE_EXDOS
  Resets EXDOS's drive state (EXDOS_STATE_RST with A), offers the command at DE to the
  extensions (EXOS 26) and leaves action 8 to the others (C = 8).
.0203 Reset the state
.0207 Offer the command
.0209 C = 8: the other extensions get action 8 too

@0221 REINIT
  Action 8 again (EXDOS initialised before).
  1. Keeps bits 7, 6, 2 and 1 of IY-$37.
  2. Links the DISK device and the buffers again unless the device for drive A answers
     (RELINK_DEVICES), and the drive letter devices.
  3. Calls function 1 of every driver block in the chain at IY+$1E (REINIT_DRV_LP).
.0221 Keep bits 7, 6, 2, 1 of the flags
.0226 Relink the devices
.0229 Keep B (1: nothing relinked)
.022A A = the first driver block's segment
.022D HL = its address

@0233 REINIT_DRV_LP
  Calls function 1 of each driver block in the chain (link: segment, address in the block's
  first 3 bytes; segment 0 ends it), then frees the scratch buffer (FN30_SCRATCH_BUF with
  A = B = 0) and announces "EXDOS" + $FB with A = the B kept at REINIT.
.0233 Page the block in
.0236 HL = its first unit record
.0239 Driver function 1
.023F A = the next block's segment
.0241 HL = its address
.0246 Another block?
.0248 B = 0, A = 0: release
.0249 Free the scratch buffer
.024C DE = the command "EXDOS" + $FB
.024F A = the B kept at REINIT

@0252 RELINK_DEVICES
  Checks whether the device for drive A answers (QUERY_DRIVE_DEV); if it does, returns with
  B = 1. Otherwise links the DISK device and the buffers again (LINK_DISK_DEV) and goes on
  at RELINK_LETTERS (B = 0).
  Entry: IY = the workspace.
  Exit: A = 0, Z; B = 1 when nothing was linked, 0 when the devices were linked again.
  Error: none (a failed link is not reported).
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL, BC', DE', HL' (RELINK_LET_DEV works in the alternate set).
  Keeps: IX, IY.
.0252 Drive A
.0257 B = 1: nothing relinked
.0259 Device A answers? Done
.025A Link DISK and the buffers again

@025D RELINK_LETTERS
  Makes sure every drive from 26 down to 1 that has a unit has its letter device
  (ENSURE_DRIVE_DEV); a unit record with byte +9 not 0 gets 1 there.
.025D From drive 26 down

@025F RELINK_LET_LP
  One drive: maps it to its unit and pages the unit's record in.
.025F A = the drive
.0260 HL = its unit's slot
.0263 A = the slot's segment
.0265 No unit? Next drive
.0267 Page the record in
.0269 DE = the record's address
.026D Its byte +9
.0273 0? Keep it
.0275 Else 1

@0277 RELINK_LET_DEV
  Links drive B's letter device unless it answers.
.0277 A = the drive
.0278 Keep B, the loop counter
.0279 Link the letter device if needed

@027D RELINK_LET_NXT
  Next drive; A = 0 when all are done.
.027F A = 0, Z

@0281 LINK_DISK_DEV
  Links the DISK device (the descriptor copied to IY+$01FB) with EXOS 21, empties the buffer
  list, then links the buffer device "." with as many sector buffers as IY+$21 asks
  (LINK_DRIVE_DEV), falling back to 3 when the RAM is not there.
  Entry: IY = the workspace; IY+$21 = the buffers wanted.
  Exit: Z when the buffers were linked; IY+$21 = their count.
  Error: NZ and carry when not even 3 buffers could be linked.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL; IY+$21-$27.
  Keeps: IX, IY.
.0281 HL = IY+$229, 3 below the second descriptor's copy
.0288 Kept for LINK_DRIVE_DEV
.028E HL - 14: the DISK descriptor's DD_TYPE
.0293 No device RAM
.0298 A = 0
.0299 No buffers in the list
.029F IY+$24/$25 high byte = 0: no recent buffer
.02A2 B = the buffers wanted

@02A5 LINK_BUFS_TRY
  Links the buffer device with B buffers; when that fails and B was 4 or more, tries once
  more with 3.
.02A5 None linked yet
.02A9 The device's name: "."
.02AC Link it with B buffers
.02B0 Linked? Done
.02B2 Fewer than 4 asked? Fail (carry)
.02B5 Try with 3

@02B9 FN27_SET_BUFS
  EXDOS function 27: adds sector buffers until there are B (at most 10). Asking for fewer
  than there are changes nothing.
  Entry: B = the buffers wanted; IY = the workspace.
  Exit: A = 0; B = the buffers there are.
  Error: A = $F7 with carry for more than 10; a failed link's status.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL.
  Keeps: IX, IY.
.02B9 More than 10 buffers?
.02BC Error $F7
.02BF B = how many more are wanted
.02C4 A = 0
.02C6 None? Done
.02C8 The buffer device "."
.02CA More wanted: link them

@02CD BUFS_COUNT_RET
  Returns B = the number of buffers (IY+$21).
.02CD B = the buffers there are

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Drives and units

@02D1 DRIVE_TO_UNIT
  Maps a drive to its unit: A = the byte at IY+$2A0 + drive (0 for a drive above 26), and
  HL = that unit's slot (UNIT_SLOT).
  Entry: A = the drive (1-26); IY = the workspace.
  Exit: A = the unit's number; HL = its slot (segment, then the record's address).
  Changes: AF, HL.
  Keeps: BC, DE, IX, IY.
.02D1 Above 26?
.02D5 Taken as 0

@02D6 MAP_DRIVE_TBL
  Reads the drive's entry in the map at IY+$2A0 and finds the slot of the unit it names.
.02DA HL = the map, IY+$2A0
.02DF HL = the drive's entry
.02E3 A = its unit
.02E5 HL = the unit's slot
.02E8 A = the unit

@02EB UNIT_PAGE_IN
  Pages a unit's record in: A = the unit, its slot gives the segment (put in page 1) and the
  record's address.
  Entry: A = the unit (1-26); IY = the workspace.
  Exit: page 1 = the record's segment (also in A); DE = the record's address; HL = the
  slot's address high byte.
  Error: Z (A = 0) when the slot is empty.
  Changes: AF, DE, HL; page 1.
  Keeps: BC, IX, IY.
.02EB HL = the unit's slot
.02EE A = its segment
.02F0 No unit? Z
.02F1 Page the record in
.02F4 DE = the record's address

@02F8 UNIT_SLOT
  Finds a unit's slot: HL = IY+$24F + 3 x unit (a unit above 26 is taken as 0). A slot is the
  segment of the unit's record (0: no unit) and the record's address.
  Entry: A = the unit; IY = the workspace.
  Exit: HL = the slot.
  Changes: AF, DE, HL.
  Keeps: BC, IX, IY.
.02F8 Above 26?
.02FC Taken as 0

@02FD UNIT_SLOT_ADDR
  HL = IY+$24F + 3 x A.
.02FD A = unit x 3
.0303 HL = the slot table (unit 1 at IY+$252)

@030C QUERY_DRIVE_DEV
  Asks whether the device named after drive A answers: builds the name "<letter>:" (counted)
  at IY+$6D1 and opens it in query mode (OPEN_QUERY).
  Entry: A = the drive (1-26); IY = the workspace.
  Exit: A = 0; Z when the open ended with status $F9 or $F8.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL.
  Keeps: IX, IY.
.030C A = the drive's letter
.030E HL = IY+$6D3, the name's end
.0315 ":"
.0318 E = the letter (L after the swap: bit 7 clear, open)
.0319 The letter
.031B The name's length: 2
.031D DE = the name
.031E Open it in query mode
.0321 Status $F9?
.0324 Z when the status was $F8
.0326 A = 0

@0329 FN23_OPEN_DISK
  EXDOS function 23: opens a channel to "DISK:" in query mode, passing BC to the device in
  IY+$6B/$6C; falls into OPEN_QUERY.
.0329 DE = "DISK:"
.032C BC for the device

@0332 OPEN_QUERY
  Opens a channel (OPEN_FREE_CHAN) with bit 0 of IY-$37 set while it runs, so EXDOS's own
  devices know the open is a query.
  Entry: DE = the device name (counted); L bit 7 = 0; IY = the workspace.
  Exit: A = the last status, flags from OR A; DE = the word at IY+$6B; B = IY+$6D.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, H.
.0332 Query mode on

@0336 OPEN_FREE_CHAN
  Opens a channel to the name at DE on the first free channel number from 0 up: EXOS 1
  (open), or EXOS 2 (create) when entered at $0339, which sets bit 7 of L. Status $F9 moves
  on to the next number; any other error closes the channel. Ends with bit 0 of IY-$37
  clear, B = IY+$6D and DE = the word at IY+$6B.
  Entry: DE = the name (counted); L = flags; IY = the workspace.
  Exit: A = the status, flags from OR A; H = the channel tried last; DE = the word at
  IY+$6B; B = IY+$6D.
  Error: A = the error's status (NZ); $F9 when every number was taken.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, H; bit 7 of L.
.0336 Open (bit 7 of L clear)
.0338 Its bytes CB FD: SET 7,L, the create entry $0339
.033B From channel 0

@033D OPEN_TRY_CHAN
  Tries channel H: EXOS 1 (open) or, when bit 7 of L is set, EXOS 2 (create).
.033D A = the channel
.033F Create?
.0345 Its bytes F7 02: EXOS 2 (create), the entry at $0346
.0349 Opened? Done
.034B Status $F9: the channel number is taken?
.034F Try the next number
.0352 All taken: give up with $F9

@0354 OPEN_CLOSE_FAIL
  Any other error: closes channel H and keeps the error's status.
.0355 A = the channel

@0359 QUERY_OPEN_END
  Leaves query mode and returns B = IY+$6D, DE = the word at IY+$6B, A = the status.
.0359 Query mode off
.035D B = IY+$6D

@0360 DE_FROM_IY6B
  DE = the word at IY+$6B; returns with flags from OR A.
  Entry: A; IY = the workspace.
  Exit: DE = the word at IY+$6B; flags from A.
  Changes: F, DE.
  Keeps: A, BC, HL, IX, IY.
.0360 DE = (IY+$6B)

@0364 OR_A_RET
  Returns with the flags from A (IY_WORD_INLINE comes back here past its byte).
.0364 Flags from A, carry clear

@036C ENSURE_DRIVE_DEV
  Makes sure drive A has its letter device: when the query of "<letter>:" ends with status
  $F9 or $F8 nothing is done; otherwise the device named after the letter is linked with no
  buffers (LINK_DRIVE_DEV).
  Entry: A = the drive (1-26); IY = the workspace.
  Exit: Z, A = 0 when the device answered or was linked.
  Error: NZ, A = the status when the link failed.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL.
  Keeps: IX, IY.
.036C Does the device answer?
.036F Yes: nothing to do
.0370 C = the letter, the device's name

@0372 LINK_DRIVE_DEV
  Links a device whose name is the one character in C, with RAM for B sector buffers of $20B
  bytes and a header (B x $20B + 14 bytes), from the descriptor template 3 bytes above the
  address at IY+$26. The template's 9 bytes are then copied below the device's RAM (whose
  address is at IY+$6B), IY+$26 points 3 below the copy, and the two bytes under it hold the
  workspace's distance (IY - copy - 1). With B not 0 the buffers are linked into the list at
  IY+$22 (LINK_BUF_LOOP).
  Entry: B = the buffers; C = the name; IY = the workspace.
  Exit: Z, A = 0 when linked; IY+$21 counts the buffers linked.
  Error: NZ, A = EXOS 21's status.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL; IY+$21-$27.
.0373 A = the name
.0374 $FE03 + $20B = $000E: the header
.0377 + $20B per buffer
.037A B + 1 additions
.037E BC = the RAM wanted
.0380 DE = (IY+$26)

@037B LINK_DEV_SIZE
  Adds $20B per buffer.
.037B + one buffer

@0384 LINK_DEV_DONE
  Second half of LINK_DRIVE_DEV: links the device, then copies its descriptor below its RAM.
.0384 DE = the template's DD_TYPE
.0387 HL = the name's character
.038B The device's name
.038C Link it, BC bytes of RAM
.038E B = the buffers
.038F Failed? Return its status
.0390 A = the buffers
.0391 DE = (IY+$6B): the device's RAM
.0394 The 9 bytes below it
.0398 Copy the descriptor there
.039D HL = IY - the copy - 1
.03A0 DE = that distance, HL = below the copy
.03A3 IY+$26 = 3 below the copy
.03AA The distance below that
.03AD No buffers?

@03B0 LINK_BUF_LOOP
  Puts one buffer ($20C bytes below the last) at the head of the buffer list.
.03B0 HL = the next buffer, $20C lower
.03B4 DE = (IY+$22), the list's head

@03B8 LINK_BUF_NEXT
  Second half of LINK_BUF_LOOP: the buffer becomes the head, its link the old head.
.03B8 The new head
.03BE Its link: the old head
.03C1 One more buffer
.03C4 More?

@03C8 FN24_ADD_DRIVER
  EXDOS function 24: adds the units of a driver. The block at DE (in segment B, read in page
  1) holds the number of units, the driver's routine address and its segment, then room
  for a record of $60 bytes per unit; ADD_UNITS builds the records over it.
  Entry: B = the block's segment; DE = its address; IY = the workspace.
  Exit: A = 0, Z when the block has no units; else as ADD_UNITS.
  Error: Z when there are no units (nothing added).
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL, IX; page 1.
.03C8 Page the block in
.03CB IX = the block's address: the driver's IY
.03CE In page 1
.03D3 A = the number of units
.03D5 None? Done
.03D6 C = the count
.03D9 DE = the driver's routine
.03DD A = the driver's segment

@03DF ADD_UNITS
  Adds C units of a driver.
  1. For each unit, writes a record ($60 bytes, the first at HL + 3): +0 the segment for
     page 1 (B), +1/+2 the driver's IY (IX), +3 its segment (A), +4/+5 its routine (DE),
     +6 the unit's number within the driver (from 0), +7 and +8 the drive given to it,
     +9 = 0, +$1F = $FF.
  2. Gives it the first free drive from 1 to 26: the drive's slot gets the record's segment
     and address (interrupts off while it is written), and the letter device is linked
     (ENSURE_DRIVE_DEV). A unit gets no drive when all 26 are taken.
  3. Links the block at the head of the chain at IY+$1E-$20 (its first 3 bytes get the old
     head) and calls the driver's function 0 for its first unit (DRV_CALL_FF).
  Entry: HL = the block; C = the units; DE = the routine; A = the driver's segment; B = the
  block's segment; IX = the value the driver gets in IY; IY = the workspace.
  Exit: A = the status of driver function 0, flags from OR A.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL, AF'; page 1.
.03DF A' = 0: the first unit's number
.03E2 The block's address in page 1
.03E6 Keep the block's start
.03E7 The first record at +3

@03EA ADD_UNIT_LP
  Writes one unit's record and looks for a free drive for it.
.03ED +0: the segment for page 1
.03EE BC = the driver's IY
.03F4 +1/+2: the driver's IY
.03F8 +3: the driver's segment
.03FA +4/+5: the routine
.03FE +6: the unit's number in the driver
.0400 Next unit's number
.0403 Keep +7, where the drive goes
.0406 +9 = 0
.0408 + $16
.040C +$1F = $FF
.040E 26 drives
.0410 HL = drive 1's slot, IY+$252
.0417 DE = the record's +7

@0418 ADD_UNIT_FREE
  Looks for the first drive whose slot is empty.
.0418 A = the slot's segment
.041A Empty? This drive
.041D Next slot
.0421 None free: no drive for the unit

@0423 ADD_UNIT_DRIVE
  Gives the unit drive 27 - B: writes it in the record (+7 and +8) and the record's segment
  and address in the drive's slot, then links the drive's letter device.
.0423 A = the drive's number
.0426 Record +7
.0428 And +8
.0429 C = the drive
.042A No interrupts while the slot changes
.042B DE = the record
.042D A = the record's segment
.042F The slot's address
.0434 The slot's segment
.0436 A = the drive
.0437 Link its letter device if needed

@043A ADD_UNIT_NEXT
  Next record ($60 bytes on); after the last, links the block into the chain at IY+$1E and
  calls driver function 0.
.043A HL = this record
.043B The next record
.0444 One unit fewer
.0447 HL = IY+$1E, the chain's head
.044E DE = the block
.0450 Its first 3 bytes = the old head
.0455 BC = the block
.0457 The head = the block's address
.045B And segment (page 1)
.045E HL = the first record's +8
.0462 B = its drive
.0463 HL = the first record
.0464 Driver function 0

@0465 DRV_CALL_FF
  Calls a driver function (DRIVER_CALL) with C = $FF, so page 1 holds segment $FF during
  the call, and IX = IY + $C4A1.
.0465 IX = IY + $C4A1
.046E Segment $FF for page 1

@0470 DRIVER_CALL
  Calls a unit's driver through the RAM copy of RAM_FARCALL. The unit's record gives: +0 the
  value for B', +1/+2 for IY, +3 the segment for page 3, +4/+5 the routine, +6 the value
  for C.
  1. Keeps IX, IY, HL, the alternate set and AF'; IY = the RAM code (IY+$01ED), whose
     page-1 restore gets page 1's segment and whose page-3 load the record's segment.
  2. Pages segment C into page 1 and jumps to RAM_FARCALL with the routine in HL, the
     record's IY on the stack and A' = the entry A.
  3. RAM_FARCALL returns to DRV_CALL_RET with the driver's status in A'.
  Entry: A = the function code; HL = the unit's record; C = the segment for page 1; DE for
  the driver; IY = the workspace.
  Exit: A = the driver's status, flags from OR A ($E5 when SOFT_IRQ_CHECK finds $20); BC, DE
  as the driver left them; pages 1 and 3 restored.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE.
  Keeps: HL, IX, IY.
.0475 Swap to keep the alternates and A' = A
.047A Keep AF' (the caller's)
.047C Keep DE
.047D IY = the RAM code
.0482 Page 1's segment, restored after the call
.0487 A = record +0
.048A Into B'
.048C DE = record +1/+2: the driver's IY
.0490 For RAM_FARCALL's POP IY
.0491 Record +3: the driver's segment
.0492 Into the RAM code's page-3 load
.0496 DE = record +4/+5: the routine
.049A A = the segment for page 1
.049B C = record +6
.049C Page it in
.049E HL = the routine
.049F To the RAM copy of RAM_FARCALL

@04A1 DRV_CALL_RET
  The return from a driver call: A = the driver's status (or $E5), the caller's AF', BC',
  DE', HL', HL, IY and IX back, flags from OR A.
.04A1 $20 pending? A' = $E5
.04A4 The caller's AF'
.04A5 A = the status
.04B0 Flags from the status

@04B2 SOFT_IRQ_CHECK
  Checks $BFF2 (EXOS 2.1's FLAG_SOFT_IRQ) for $20: if it is there, A' = $E5 and Z.
  Entry: none.
  Exit: Z and A' = $E5 when $BFF2 holds $20; NZ otherwise, A' unchanged.
  Changes: AF, AF'.
  Keeps: BC, DE, HL, IX, IY.
.04B2 A = the pending software interrupt
.04B5 $20?
.04B8 Status $E5 instead
.04BB Z

@04BD RAM_FARCALL
  Code copied to IY+$01ED at INIT_ACTION and run there (the operands at +4, +9, +$0D and
  +$11 are patched): DRIVER_CALL's far call. Pops the driver's IY and the caller's DE,
  pages the driver's segment into page 3, calls the routine in HL with A = the function
  code, restores pages 1 and 3 and goes back to DRV_CALL_RET with the status in A'.
.04BD IY = the driver's IY
.04BF The caller's DE
.04C0 The driver's segment (patched)
.04C2 Into page 3
.04C4 A = the function code
.04C5 Call HL (patched to the copy's JP (HL))
.04C8 A' = the status
.04C9 Page 1's segment (patched)
.04CD EXDOS's segment (patched)
.04CF Back into page 3
.04D1 Back in EXDOS

@04D4 RAM_JP_HL
  The copy's JP (HL): both copied CALLs come here.
.04D4 To the routine

@04D5 RAM_SEGCALL
  Also run in the copy (IY+$0205, called at $0F0C): calls DE with segment A in page 3 and
  A = IY+$2A, then puts EXDOS's segment (EXDOS variable $40) back in page 3.
.04D5 Segment A into page 3
.04D7 A = IY+$2A for the routine
.04DA HL = the routine
.04DB Call it (patched to the copy's JP (HL))
.04DE B = its result
.04DF EXDOS's segment
.04E4 A = the result

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The function dispatcher

@0505 FUNC_DISPATCH
  EXDOS function call (entry $C010): runs function A (1-30) from the table at $0550 with the
  caller's BC, DE, HL.
  1. Keeps the alternate BC, DE, HL and AF', page 1's segment (IY+$2D) and SP (IY+$28, for
     the error handler's abandon), clears IY+$48.
  2. Enters the function through FUNC_JUMP with A = 0; it returns to FUNC_RETURN.
  Entry: A = the function; BC, DE, HL its parameters; IY = the workspace.
  Exit: A = the status, flags from OR A; BC, DE, HL as the function left them; page 1, BC',
  DE', HL' and AF' restored.
  Error: A = $B0 (NZ) for function 0 or above 30.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL, IX.
.0505 Above 30?
.0509 Use the alternate set; the caller's in the other
.050A Keep the caller's alternates
.050D BC = the function x 2
.0511 A' = the function x 2
.0512 Keep the caller's AF'
.0513 Keep page 1's segment
.0518 IY+$48 = 0
.051B HL = SP
.051E Kept for an abandoned call
.0524 HL = the function's table entry
.0528 DE = the function's address
.052B A = 0
.052C Enter it; it returns to FUNC_RETURN

@052F FUNC_RETURN
  The return from an EXDOS function (also where the error handler abandons one, with the
  stack put back): restores page 1, checks $BFF2, restores the caller's AF', BC', DE', HL',
  clears bit 1 of IY-$37 and IY-$80, and returns A = the status with flags from OR A.
.052F A' = the status
.0530 Page 1 as it was
.0535 $20 pending? Status $E5
.0538 The caller's AF'
.0539 A = the status
.053F Clear bit 1 of the flags
.0543 IY-$80 = 0
.0547 Flags from the status

@0549 FUNC_JUMP
  Jumps to DE with the caller's BC, DE, HL back in the main set.
  Entry: DE = the address (alternate set active).
  Exit: none: it jumps.
  Changes: the main and alternate sets swap.
.0549 The address as the return
.054A The caller's registers
.054B To the function

@054C FUNC_BAD
  Function 0 or a number above 30: A = $B0, NZ.
  Entry: none.
  Exit: A = $B0; NZ, carry clear.
  Changes: AF.
  Keeps: BC, DE, HL, IX, IY.
.054C Status $B0

@058E FN28_DRIVE_E
  EXDOS function 28: maps drive 5 (E:) to itself (FN26_MAP_DRIVE), selects it and drops its
  buffers (SELECT_DROP), calls its driver with A = $FF (DRIVER_CALL, C = 5); with B not 0
  goes on at $05BC.
  Entry: B = the parameter; DE; IY = the workspace.
  Exit: A = 0, Z when B is 0; else as $05BC.
  Error: NZ, A = the status from selecting the drive or from the driver.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL, IX.
.058E Drive 5
.0591 B = C = 5: drive 5 to itself
.0596 Select it, drop its buffers
.0599 Failed?
.059B Driver function $FF
.05A1 Failed?
.05A2 B = 0? Done

@0608 SELECT_DROP
  Selects drive C (SELECT_DRIVE with B = 2: its changed buffers written back), drops its
  buffers (BUF_DROP_DRIVE) and resets the record (+9 = 0, +$1E = $FF at $0DE6).
  Entry: C = the drive; DE; IY = the workspace.
  Exit: A = 0, Z; HL = the drive's record; IX = DE.
  Error: NZ, A = the status from SELECT_DRIVE.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL, IX.
.060A B = 2
.060C Select the drive
.0610 IX = the caller's DE
.0613 Failed?
.0614 Drop its buffers
.0617 Reset the record

@0AB9 FN30_SCRATCH_BUF
  EXDOS function 30: with B = $FF gets a buffer for sector A x $101 tagged $80 (a scratch
  buffer, read in when bit 0 of A is set) and returns its data's address; with any other B
  gets it and frees it (tag 0). The drive record GET_SECTOR_BUF reads is the code at $0AC9,
  so its fields are this code's bytes (+8 = $80, the tag).
  Entry: A = the sector byte (also the flags); B = $FF to keep the buffer; IY = the
  workspace.
  Exit: A = 0; with B = $FF, HL = the buffer's data (+$0B) and Z; else HL = the buffer's
  tag, NZ.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL.
.0ABA IX = $0AC9: a record made of code bytes
.0ABE DE = A x $101: the sector
.0AC0 B = the flags
.0AC1 HL = the buffer
.0AC5 A = 0
.0AC6 B = $FF? Keep it
.0AC9 HL = the buffer's tag
.0ACB Free it

@0AE0 GET_SECTOR_BUF
  Finds or loads a sector into a buffer with one sector (IY+$68 = 1); $0AE2 continues.
  1. The tag is the record's +8 (C): the most recent buffer (IY+$24) or one in the list at
     IY+$22 that holds the same tag and sector is used as it is.
  2. Otherwise the first buffer of the list (the second when the first is a scratch one,
     tag bit 7) is written back if changed and filled in: +2 the tag, +3 bit 1 of B, +4/+5
     the sector, +6 the count, +7 the record's +$11, +8 page 1's segment.
  3. With bit 0 of B set the sector is read through the driver (function 4); a failed read
     goes to the error handler ($0ED5), which retries, goes on or abandons the call.
  4. The buffer becomes the most recent one (IY+$24).
  Entry: IX = the drive's record; DE = the sector; B = the flags (bit 0: read it); IY = the
  workspace.
  Exit: HL = the buffer; A = 0, Z.
  Error: a failed read reaches the error handler, which may abandon the EXDOS call.
  Changes: AF, BC, HL.
  Keeps: DE.
.0AE0 One sector

@0BBD BUF_DROP_DRIVE
  Drops a drive's buffers: marks them (BUF_MARK_DRIVE), then unlinks each marked buffer
  through $09FC and frees it (tag 0). Entered here the tag is the record's +8 (HL); entered
  at $0BBE (SCF) it is A.
  Entry: HL = the drive's record, or A = the tag at $0BBE; IY = the workspace.
  Exit: Z; HL kept.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE.
  Keeps: HL.
.0BBD Carry clear: the tag from the record; $0BBE: SCF
.0BBF Mark the drive's buffers

@0BD1 BUF_MARK_DRIVE
  Marks the buffers of one drive: bit 0 of each buffer's flags (+3) is set when its tag (+2)
  is the drive's, and cleared otherwise. Tag $FF marks every buffer in use whose tag is
  below $80; free buffers (tag 0) are never marked.
  Entry: carry set: A = the tag; carry clear: HL = the drive's record (the tag at +8); IY =
  the workspace.
  Exit: A = 0; Z, carry clear.
  Error: none.
  Changes: AF.
  Keeps: BC, DE, HL, IX, IY.
.0BD1 Tag given in A?

@0CC9 SELECT_DRIVE
  Selects drive C for an operation: maps it to its unit (kept in IY+$2B) and pages the unit's
  record in. The byte at IY+$234 + record +7 names the unit that last used that physical
  unit; when it is another one, that one's changed buffers are written back and the error
  handler is called with $B1 for it. The unit then takes the physical unit over. B says
  what follows ($0CFD on): 2 writes back the drive's changed buffers and returns A = 0,
  HL = the record.
  Entry: C = the drive; B = the operation; IY = the workspace.
  Exit: A = 0; HL = the record (B = 2).
  Error: A = $AF, Z when the drive has no unit.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL, IX.
.0CC9 A = the drive
.0CCA HL = its unit's slot
.0CCD C = the unit
.0CCE Kept in IY+$2B
.0CD1 A = the record's segment
.0CD2 Page it in
.0CD4 No unit?
.0CD5 Error $AF
.0CD8 DE = the record
.0CDD Its +7: the physical unit
.0CE4 HL = IY+$234 + that
.0CEC A = the unit that used it last
.0CED This one? Go on
.0CF0 None? Go on
.0CF3 Write back the other one's buffers
.0CF6 Code $B1 for the error handler
.0CF8 HL = the record, the stacked HL kept
.0CF9 Report it for that record

@0ED4 DRIVE_ERROR
  The drive error handler entered with carry set (IY+$2A = $FF); $0ED5 takes the carry from
  the caller. It reports code A for the drive record at HL (through the error hook at IY-$3A
  when one is set) and acts on the answer: it returns Z to retry, NZ to go on, or abandons
  the EXDOS call through FUNC_RETURN.
  Entry: A = the code; HL = the drive's record; IY = the workspace.
  Exit: Z: retry; NZ: go on.
  Changes: AF.
  Keeps: BC, DE, HL, IX, IY.
.0ED4 IY+$2A will be $FF

@0DB9 FN25_COPY_DRIVE
  EXDOS function 25: makes drive B use drive C's unit. Copies the first 8 bytes of C's record
  (the driver call: segments, IY, routine, unit number, physical unit) to IY+$6A1, writes
  back and drops drive B's buffers, copies the 8 bytes over B's record and resets it (+9 =
  0, +$1E = $FF at $0DE6).
  Entry: C = the drive to copy; B = the drive that becomes a copy; IY = the workspace.
  Exit: A = 0, Z.
  Error: A = $A2 (Z) when either drive has no unit.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL; page 1.
.0DB9 HL = IY+$6A1, an 8-byte store
.0DC1 A = the drive to copy
.0DC2 DE = its record
.0DC5 HL = the record
.0DC6 DE = the store
.0DC7 Error $A2: no unit
.0DCB A = the drive to change
.0DCC 8 bytes
.0DD2 Write back its changed buffers
.0DD5 Drop them
.0DD8 A = the drive to change
.0DD9 DE = its record
.0DDC HL = the store
.0DDD Error $A2: no unit
.0DE1 8 bytes
.0DE5 HL = the changed record

@0DF4 FN26_MAP_DRIVE
  EXDOS function 26: maps drive B to the unit of drive C (C = 0: to its own), then links
  drive B's letter device if it does not answer (ENSURE_DRIVE_DEV). B = 0 maps every drive
  to itself.
  Entry: B = the drive (0: all); C = the drive whose unit it takes (0: its own); IY = the
  workspace.
  Exit: A = 0, Z (ENSURE_DRIVE_DEV's result when B is not 0).
  Error: A = $AF, carry clear, for a drive above 26.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL.
.0DF4 HL = the drive map, IY+$2A0
.0DFB A = the drive
.0DFC 0: map every drive to itself
.0DFF Above 26?
.0E01 Error $AF
.0E04 HL = the drive's entry
.0E08 A = the unit's drive
.0E09 0: its own
.0E0C Above 26?
.0E0E Error $AF
.0E11 B = the new unit

@2D74 EXDOS_STATE_RST
  Resets EXDOS's drive state: $FF into IY-$2F, -$30, -$31 and EXDOS variables $4D and $4E
  (IY-$51, IY-$50); with A not 0 and bit 7 of IY-$2B set, also calls $2F85; then $2D60.
  Entry: A = 0 on the first initialisation; IY = the workspace.
  Exit: as $2D60.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL.
.2D74 HL = IY-$2F
.2D7B $FF (D)
.2D80 EXDOS variable $4D = $FF
.2D83 EXDOS variable $4E = $FF
.2D86 First initialisation?
.2D89 Flag bit 7 of IY-$2B: call $2F85 first?

@3FB2 ANNOUNCE_DRIVES
  Offers "EXDOS E:", "EXDOS F:", "EXDOS B:" and "EXDOS A:" to the extensions in turn
  (EXDOS_DRV_CMD), building each at IY+$5DB.
  Entry: IY = the workspace.
  Exit: A = the last status; EXDOS variable $47 = 1.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL.
  Keeps: IX, IY.
.3FB2 HL = IY+$5DB, the buffer
.3FB9 Drive 5 (E:)
.3FBE Drive 6 (F:)
.3FC3 Drive 2 (B:)
.3FC8 Drive 1 (A:)

@3FCA EXDOS_DRV_CMD
  Makes drive A the current one (EXDOS variable $47) and offers the command "EXDOS
  <letter>:" to the extensions (EXOS 26), built from the text at $0131 in the buffer at HL.
  Entry: A = the drive (1-26); HL = a 9-byte buffer; IY = the workspace.
  Exit: A = EXOS 26's status; HL = the buffer.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE.
  Keeps: HL, IX, IY.
.3FCA EXDOS variable $47: the current drive
.3FCD The text "EXDOS A:"
.3FD0 HL = the text, DE = the buffer
.3FD5 Copy it
.3FD7 A = the drive's letter
.3FD9 DE = the letter's place
.3FDB Over the "A"
.3FDE Offer the command

question 0321 What do statuses $F9 and $F8 mean when "<letter>:" is opened in query mode?
question 0465 Is IX = IY + $C4A1 meant, and does a driver read IX in functions 0 and 1?
question 2D92 Nothing reaches $2D92-$2D99 after the JR at $2D90: dead code or a hidden entry?
question 0ED4 Which answers of the error hook mean abandon, retry and go on (B = 1, 2, 3)?

feedback missing 00F2-0104 CALL $00F2 returns past its inline byte (EX (SP)); the cards for $0360, $0380, $03B4 and $0F07 say "return cannot be followed" and list $0364, $0384, $03B8 as unreached, though they are the continuations
feedback missing 013A-01A1 The 72 bytes $04BD-$0504 are copied to IY+$01ED and patched; JP (IY) at $049F and the CALL at $0F0C run that copy, so $04BD-$04E5 is RAM code and $04E6-$0504 two device descriptor templates
feedback wrong 01D1 "in from: jump $0B78" is false: the JP Z at $0B78 follows a JR Z on the same flag and never jumps; its bytes hide POP DE/POP BC at $0B79
feedback pattern 01B2-01CA Driver routines are passed as DE to ADD_UNITS ($1FD3, $1005) and called through DRIVER_CALL; their cards say unreached
feedback wrong 2D74 The card's body includes $2D92-$2D99, which follow an unconditional JR and are reached from nowhere on the card
feedback missing 0013-0017 IY is the workspace + $80 from $0017 on, and ACT4_VAR shows IY-$5E..IY-$3B are EXDOS's own EXOS variables $40-$63; a card fact naming those would save reading the variable code each time
feedback pattern 0252-0372 The Changes questions for IX, IY and the alternates on $0252, $0281, $02B9, $030C and $036C come from the unfollowed returns of CALL $00F2; no path through them uses EXX (except RELINK_LET_DEV) or loads IX/IY
